.TH "tum\-keymap" "5" "" "" "tmux\-borrow Manual"
.SS NAME
tum\-keymap [em] the tum key\-binding scheme and the shipped
\f[CR]tum\-bindings.conf\f[R]
.SS SYNOPSIS
\f[CR]source\-file \[ti]/.config/tmux/tum\-bindings.conf\f[R]
.SS DESCRIPTION
A shortcut\-driven way to drive every \f[B]tum\f[R](1) command, grouped
so it is memorizable.
The keymap is a thin layer over the CLI: every binding shells out to the
same \f[CR]tum\f[R] commands scripts use, so the keymap and the CLI can
never drift apart.
The bindings ship as a single static file,
\f[CR]tum\-bindings.conf\f[R], that you source from your
\f[CR]tmux.conf\f[R]; to remap a key you edit that file directly.
There is no generator and no per\-key option.
.PP
The design goals, in priority order:
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Fast for the daily loop.\f[R] Borrow, flip\-flop, and close must be
reflexes.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]Collision\-free with tmux.\f[R] Nothing steals tmux\[cq]s own
prefix bindings.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Memorizable.\f[R] Commands grouped under mnemonic leaders;
uppercase = stronger variant; one mental map covers everything.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]Complete.\f[R] Every command is reachable from the keyboard.
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]Configurable.\f[R] Every key is remappable; the bindings live in
one editable file.
.SS THE CHAIN
There are two entry styles, and they coexist:
.IP
.EX
<prefix>  H  <slot>          \->  flip\-flop (direct)
<prefix>  t  <key>           \->  the other verbs (grouped, collision\-safe)
<prefix>  t  <group> <key>   \->  a family action
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]<prefix>\f[R] is your normal tmux prefix (default \f[CR]C\-b\f[R];
many users remap to \f[CR]C\-a\f[R] or \f[CR]Space\f[R]).
The keymap never assumes a particular prefix.
.PP
\f[CR]H\f[R] enters the \f[CR]tum\-flip\f[R] sub\-table directly from
the prefix table.
The next key is a slot (\f[CR]0\f[R][en]\f[CR]9\f[R],
\f[CR]a\f[R][en]\f[CR]z\f[R]), or \f[CR]Enter\f[R] (context\-aware:
toggle the focused pane, or open the chooser when the host has several).
.PP
\f[CR]t\f[R] enters the \f[CR]tum\-root\f[R] key\-table.
For leaves, the action fires and you return to normal.
For group leaders, you drop into a sub\-table for one more key.
.PP
The shortest path to the most common action is two keystrokes:
.IP
.EX
C\-b   H   1      \->   flip\-flop slot 1 (hide if shown, show if hidden)
.EE
.PP
Borrow is three:
.IP
.EX
C\-b   t   b      \->   borrow (interactive chooser)
.EE
.SS DESIGN PRINCIPLES
These rules generate the whole keymap.
Once you internalize them, you can derive any binding without looking it
up.
.IP "1." 3
\f[B]Flip\-flop is bound directly to \f[CB]H\f[B].\f[R] \f[CR]H\f[R]
(capital) is collision\-safe [em] tmux has no default
\f[CR]prefix H\f[R] binding [em] so it lives directly in the prefix
table.
.IP "2." 3
\f[B]The \f[CB]t\f[B] leader scopes the remaining verbs.\f[R] The
natural mnemonic keys for the other verbs collide with tmux defaults
(\f[CR]x\f[R] = kill\-pane, \f[CR]p\f[R] = previous\-window,
\f[CR]c\f[R] = new\-window, \f[CR]n\f[R] = next\-window, \f[CR]l\f[R] =
last\-window, \f[CR]s\f[R] = choose\-session).
After your prefix, \f[CR]t\f[R] enters \[lq]tum mode\[rq] and every
subsequent key is read from tum\[cq]s own key\-tables, so none of these
collide.
\f[CR]H\f[R] is the exception: it is safe, so it stays direct.
.IP "3." 3
\f[B]Single key = common verb; \f[CB]<group> <action>\f[B] =
family.\f[R] The things you do constantly (borrow, close, push, pop,
import) are single keys right after the leader.
Everything that manages nouns (layout, list, config) is a two\-key
chord.
.IP "4." 3
\f[B]Uppercase = stronger / broader variant.\f[R] \f[CR]B\f[R] borrows
new (vs \f[CR]b\f[R] borrow existing\-or\-create); \f[CR]P\f[R] pops (vs
\f[CR]p\f[R] push); \f[CR]I\f[R] imports named (vs \f[CR]i\f[R]
anonymous).
Capital always means \[lq]more.\[rq]
.IP "5." 3
\f[B]Mnemonics mirror the CLI verbs.\f[R] \f[CR]b\f[R]orrow,
\f[CR]x\f[R] (close/kill), \f[CR]p\f[R]ush, \f[CR]i\f[R]mport,
\f[CR]l\f[R]ayout, \f[CR]g\f[R]o (list), \f[CR]c\f[R]onfig, \f[CR]u\f[R]
(popup, the universe).
.IP "6." 3
\f[B]\f[CB]Esc\f[B]/\f[CB]q\f[B] always cancels, side\-effect free.\f[R]
From the leader, from any sub\-group, or mid\-prompt: cancel returns you
to the root key\-table with tmux state unchanged.
.SS MASTER SHORTCUT TABLE
Every binding, in one view.
\[lq]Direct\[rq] = bound in the prefix table (no \f[CR]t\f[R] leader);
\[lq]Leaf\[rq] = fires immediately after the leader; \[lq]Group\[rq] =
opens a sub\-table for one more key; \[lq]Sub\[rq] = opens a sub\-table
that reads a slot.
.PP
.TS
