.TE
.PP
\f[CR]t b\f[R] follows the default\-mode resolution: an instance already
bound to this host is reused; else a hidden pool instance is borrowed
(chooser if several); else a fresh one is created.
Close is immediate [em] no confirmation.
.PP
Worked example [em] the core loop.
You are editing in a project window and want the app log beside you,
then want it gone:
.IP
.EX
C\-b t b          # chooser: pick \[dq]logs\[dq] \-> the running tail pane appears beside your editor
# ...read, debug...
C\-b H 1          # flip\-flop slot 1 \-> the log pane hides; editor untouched
# ...need it again...
C\-b H 1          # flip\-flop slot 1 \-> the log pane shows again at its last geometry
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]tail\f[R] process never stopped.
Hide is not close [em] the pane is alive in the hidden session, one
keystroke away.
.PP
Worked example [em] hand a utility to another session:
.IP
.EX
# in session a, focused on htop:
C\-b t p          # htop pushed onto the stack; no host
# switch to session b (normal tmux):
C\-b t P          # htop pops in beside the current pane at its recorded geometry
.EE
.SS FLIP\-FLOP SUB\-TABLE [em] \f[CR]H ...\f[R]
Every borrowed thing (a utility, a layout, or a layout member) gets a
slot key from the range \f[CR]0\f[R][en]\f[CR]9\f[R] or
\f[CR]a\f[R][en]\f[CR]z\f[R], declared in its definition or
auto\-assigned on conflict.
After \f[CR]H\f[R], you press one key:
.PP
.TS
