T}
.TE
.PP
Avoid two specific traps: \f[CR]M\-t\f[R] (it is
\f[CR]transpose\-words\f[R] in bash and emacs and is used by vim\[cq]s
Meta layer), and \f[CR]C\-\[rs]\f[R] (it sends \f[CR]SIGQUIT\f[R] to the
foreground process).
.SS MODAL VS. AUTO\-RETURN
The shipped file uses \f[B]auto\-return\f[R]: each leaf fires and
returns you to \f[CR]root\f[R], so a second tum action needs another
entry keystroke.
This is the safe default.
If you chain several tum actions and want \f[B]sticky\f[R] entry (leaves
fire but you stay in \f[CR]tum\-root\f[R] until
\f[CR]Esc\f[R]/\f[CR]q\f[R]), edit \f[CR]tum\-bindings.conf\f[R]: change
the trailing \f[CR]switch\-client \-T root\f[R] on each leaf to
\f[CR]switch\-client \-T tum\-root\f[R].
The tradeoff is the usual modal risk [em] a fumbled key leaves you
\[lq]in tum mode\[rq] until \f[CR]Esc\f[R].
.SS MEMORIZATION GUIDE
If you remember nothing else, remember this:
.RS
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]H\f[B] toggles (hide/show).
\f[CB]t\f[B] for tum, then: \f[CB]b\f[B] borrow, \f[CB]x\f[B] close,
\f[CB]p\f[B]/\f[CB]P\f[B] push and pop, \f[CB]i\f[B]/\f[CB]I\f[B] import
(anonymous/named).
\f[CB]u\f[B] is the universe (popup).
The nouns you manage are \f[CB]l\f[B] \f[CB]g\f[B] \f[CB]c\f[B] (Layout,
Go/list, Config).
\f[CB]?\f[B] is help.
Capital always means more: new, pop, named.
\f[CB]Esc\f[B]/\f[CB]q\f[B] always cancels.\f[R]
.RE
.SS COVERAGE [em] EVERY COMMAND IS REACHABLE
.PP
.TS
